  "body": "## What this changes\n\n- Changes the example-managed Network Firewall policy from permissive alerting to default-deny.\n- Adds `firewall_allowed_domains` for explicit public HTTPS access using TLS SNI.\n- Uses strict drop/alert actions when the allowlist is empty, and AWS's established-flow actions when domain inspection needs the TLS handshake.\n- Keeps AWS APIs, S3/DynamoDB, and `app.gitpod.io` out of the public allowlist because their VPC endpoint routes do not traverse Network Firewall.\n- Fixes the existing custom `firewall_policy_arn` path so it does not evaluate an absent managed policy.\n- Documents the behavior and adds positive and negative Terraform tests.\n\n## Compatibility and parity\n\nThis is an intentional extension of the restricted-networking example. The public EC2 runner CloudFormation release `20260825.79` has no AWS Network Firewall resources to mirror.\n\nThe existing VPC endpoint contract from #43 is the prerequisite and is already on `main`. Deployments that rely on public egress must populate `firewall_allowed_domains` or provide `firewall_policy_arn`.\n\nA live AWS deployment was not run; the repository's source-level and mocked-plan coverage passed.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `terraform validate` and `terraform test` at the repository root\n- `terraform init -backend=false`, `validate`, and `test` for every module\n- `terraform init -backend=false` and `validate` for every example\n- restricted-networking example tests: 13 passed\n- `bash scripts/check-parity-contract.sh`\n- `bash scripts/test-metrics-audit-sync.sh`\n- `pre-commit run --all-files`\n\nNo Linear issue detected.",
